design/architecture\architectural_decisions.md:30:| Cards are serialized only at persistence boundaries. | Mutate the owned `Card` through a focused operation, then serialize its current state when the persistence batch flushes. Editor changes update only the edited field. Activity linking updates only activity references. External file changes are parsed and applied deliberately without replacing dirty local state. | Persistence remains deterministic while concurrent saves and external updates cannot discard unrelated newer values. |
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:15:  → configured delay or explicit flush
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:22:`DataService.init` creates one batcher per project. The first change starts its `react.autoCommitDelayMs` timer (30 seconds by default); later changes do not restart it. Changes are keyed by persisted path, and repeated edits replace pending content. Edits arriving during a flush form the next batch; failed commits remain pending for retry.
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:30:Card body, title, header, ordering, and policy edits update loaded files, then `CardOperations.saveFile` schedules them. All files share the batcher: editing two cards before a flush produces one request with both latest versions and a combined message.
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:52:| Repeated label edits before flush | Only the final target path and content remain. |
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:57:## Flushing and state
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:59:`DataService.flushPendingChanges` adds valid action drafts, then flushes the batch. Deleted or invalid drafts block it until repaired, recreated, or discarded.
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:61:Pending edits flush before operations that need durable current data:
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:70:Close and context-switch workflows await flushing where possible. Background browser handlers report failures and retain the batch for retry.
design/architecture\data_saving_and_commits.md:85:Callers flush first when ordering matters. Typing-driven updates must use the batcher.
